  "notes": [
    "Draft: Germanic cousins puzzle. Written forms: Old English *hūs*, Old Norse *hús*, Old High German *hūs*, and Gothic *-hūs* (in *gudhūs*, \"temple\"). The reveal may show the reconstructed Proto-Germanic ancestor \\*hūsą as extra context. Languages: English, German, Dutch, Swedish. About 85 entries.",
    "Draft: This is a cousins puzzle. *House, Haus, huis* and *hus* share an ancestor; none comes from another. *Husband* and *hustings* are different: they come directly from Old Norse *húsbóndi* (\"master of the house\") and *húsþing* (\"house assembly\"). *Hussy* is a shortening of *housewife*.",
    "Draft: Germanic languages build compounds freely, so the family is unlimited. The key lists only compounds found in a standard learner's dictionary.",
    "Draft: Distant cousins left out: *hut*, *hide* (conceal) and *hood* may share a far older root with *house*, but the link lies beyond the written evidence. The reveal can mention it. *Hose* and German *Hose* (\"trousers\") are a separate word.",
    "Draft: Danish and Norwegian *hus* are also cousins, but are not in this puzzle's language set.",
    "Draft: The German, Dutch and Swedish entries need a check by a fluent reader before use."
